{# _marks.html.j2 | the spot marks | 2026-08-13

   THE NEW YORKER SPOT, WITH THE REGISTER TAKEN OUT. The New Yorker drops a small
   drawn object into a column and that is the single most recognizable thing on
   its page. The drawing is witty, and wit is exactly what craft criterion C6
   rules out of this book: no populist register, no character where a structural
   account is available. So the technique is borrowed and the register is not.
   Every mark below is an INSTRUMENT, drawn from the book's own subject: a
   theorem, a routing junction, a dependency trace. None of them is a joke.

   That is the same rule AIOM_Voice_and_Craft_v1.md already applies to the four
   prose exemplars, applied to the design instead of the sentences.

   EACH MARK IS ABOUT THE SECTION IT HEADS, which is what keeps this from being
   decorative apparatus under standing rule 5. A mark that means something is a
   rubric device. A mark that means nothing is ornament, and would not belong.

   COLOUR IS NEVER LITERAL. Two scoped custom properties carry it, so a mark on
   the inverted band picks up --invert-fg and --invert-accent instead of --navy
   and --amber. Hardcoding --navy would have painted dark navy on a navy ground,
   which is the exact defect gate W13 caught in the theorem panel's numerals.

   Marks live in CHROME ONLY, on Dan's ruling of 2026-08-13. None of them appears
   inside <article id="chapter-text">, so gate W1 cannot be affected by any of it.
#}
{% macro mark(name) %}
<svg class="mark" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
     fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
  {% if name == "theorem" %}
  {# Antecedents stacked inside a brace, a rule, then the consequent. #}
  <path d="M17 11 h-5 v26 h5" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M22 16 h17 M22 22 h13 M22 28 h15" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M12 33 h27" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.45"/>
  <path d="M22 38 h11" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="2.4"/>

  {% elif name == "junction" %}
  {# One claim in, three ways out: cited, formalized, or CUT. Two redraws. A
     cross over the third branch crossed the very line it cancelled and turned
     the mark to mud; a stop bar then shared an angle with its own branch, so at
     2.35rem the whole thing read as one arrow. The cut route is now simply
     DASHED, which survives being small: the eye sees a route not taken without
     having to resolve any detail at all. #}
  <path d="M8 24 h11" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M19 24 L38 12 M19 24 h19" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M19 24 L38 36" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"
        stroke-dasharray="3.5 2.5"/>

  {% elif name == "parts" %}
  {# Four parts, rising. The first is where the reader is. #}
  <path d="M12 38 V22" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="2.6"/>
  <path d="M21 38 V17 M30 38 V25 M39 38 V13" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="2.6"/>
  <path d="M9 41 h33" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.45"/>

  {% elif name == "editions" %}
  {# Two carriers of one text. The bound page gets a spine, the screen gets a
     stand: without those the first draft was a rectangle inside a rectangle,
     which says nothing at all. #}
  <rect x="7" y="11" width="19" height="25" rx="1" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M12 11 V36" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.5"/>
  <rect x="24" y="18" width="17" height="13" rx="1" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M32.5 31 v4 M28 35.5 h9" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>

  {% elif name == "glossary" %}
  {# A term, and the definition that the chapter owning it must match. #}
  <path d="M12 15 h15" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="3"/>
  <path d="M12 24 h26 M12 30 h20 M12 36 h24" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>

  {% elif name == "graph" %}
  {# A trace through the dependency graph: three objects, two edges. #}
  <path d="M15 33 L25 17 M25 17 L37 29" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <circle cx="15" cy="33" r="4" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <circle cx="25" cy="17" r="4" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <circle cx="37" cy="29" r="4" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>

  {% elif name == "continuity" %}
  {# A promise made in one chapter and paid in a later one. #}
  <path d="M8 24 h32" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M20 13 V35 M33 13 V35" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.45"
        stroke-dasharray="2.5 3"/>
  <circle cx="20" cy="24" r="2.6" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <circle cx="33" cy="24" r="2.6" fill="var(--mark-accent)" stroke="none"/>

  {% elif name == "sources" %}
  {# A line of prose and the call that sends it to its source. #}
  <path d="M10 26 h20 M10 33 h26" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M34 24 v-9" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <circle cx="34" cy="13" r="2.4" fill="var(--mark-accent)" stroke="none"/>

  {% elif name == "search" %}
  {# One entry picked out of a set. The first draft was stacked rules in grey,
     which broke the family's solid weight AND repeated the glossary mark; a
     bracket selecting one line is a different instrument, not a lighter one. #}
  <path d="M18 16 h20 M18 32 h15" stroke="var(--mark-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M18 24 h20" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  <path d="M15 19 h-4 v10 h4" stroke="var(--mark-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
  {% endif %}
</svg>
{% endmacro %}
